.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt NC 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nc
.Nd TCP client
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Ss os-simple
.Nm
.Ar host port
.Ss os-real
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Ar host port
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Fl l
.Fl p Ar port
.Op Ar host
.Op Ar port
.Sh DESCRIPTION
On os-simple,
.Nm
is the project's TCP client.
It connects to
.Ar host
at
.Ar port ,
writes one chunk of standard input to the socket, reads one reply chunk,
and writes that reply to standard output.
It repeats until end of input or an error.
Transfer is half duplex: each input chunk is followed by one reply chunk.
.Pp
The host is an IPv4 address or a name resolved as described in
.Xr ping 1 .
The port parser accepts a positive decimal prefix and narrows it to
16 bits; it does not check the whole argument or enforce the port range.
.Pp
On os-real,
.Nm
is BusyBox's applet, with the options below.
.Sh OPTIONS
These options apply to the BusyBox applet on os-real:
.Bl -tag -width "-e program"
.It Fl b
Allow broadcasts.
.It Fl e Ar program
Run the program after connecting.
This option must be last.
.It Fl i Ar seconds
Delay this many seconds between lines sent.
.It Fl k
With
.Fl l
and
.Fl e ,
provide a persistent server.
.It Fl l
Listen for incoming connections.
.It Fl n
Do not resolve names through DNS.
.It Fl o Ar file
Write a hexadecimal dump of traffic to the file.
.It Fl p Ar port
Use this local port.
.It Fl s Ar address
Use this local address.
.It Fl u
Use UDP.
.It Fl v
Produce verbose output.
.It Fl w Ar seconds
Set the timeout for connections and final network reads.
.It Fl z
Use zero-I/O mode for scanning.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl nc 10.0.2.2 80
The
.Ql tests/run socket
and
.Ql tests/run tcp
suites use the project client with their hostpeer server.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
The project client prints
.Ql "usage: nc <host> <port>"
when a host or port operand is missing.
A port whose parsed value is not positive produces
.Ql "nc: invalid port" .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr busybox-networking 1 ,
.Xr ping 1 ,
.Xr socketcall 2
